[[systemd: Resource Control and Hardening]]

* systemd Resource control and hardening: limiting CPU/memory with cgroups, and sandboxing options like `ProtectSystem=` and `PrivateTmp=`.

Resource control uses cgroups (kernel control groups). Every service runs in its own cgroup, and systemd sets limits on it: memory, CPU, process count, I/O.
Hardening (sandboxing) uses kernel namespaces, capabilities, and seccomp. Directives like ProtectSystem= change what the process can see and do, so a compromised service can’t do much damage.

```bash
stat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup           # cgroup2fs = unified cgroup v2 (default on modern Ubuntu)
systemd-cgls                         # tree of all cgroups: slices -> services -> processes
systemd-cgtop                        # live per-cgroup CPU/memory (q to quit)
systemctl status cron.service        # the "CGroup:" line shows where it lives
systemctl show cron.service -p MemoryMax -p CPUQuotaPerSecUSec -p TasksMax -p MemoryCurrent
```